  "item_one_liners": {},
  "label": "Omics Statistics And Normalization",
  "related": [
    "sequencing-genome-dba8",
    "spatial-graph-12ac",
    "resolving-significance-5c78"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [
    "single-cell"
  ],
  "slug": "fungal-calibrated-8715",
  "summary": "Scale and compositional misspecification distort omics significance tests, prompting alternative null and scale models for count-based data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that statistical conclusions in omics depend on the correctness of the underlying data model. Two published studies address this directly for count data: a proportionality analysis quantifies bias in empirical metrics on count compositional data and proposes logit-normal multinomial model estimates, while a simulation study shows that scale misspecification inflates false discovery rates in high-throughput sequencing differential expression tools, with ALDEx2 and ALDEx3 scale models reducing this effect. The remaining preprints extend the theme more loosely: TCRdenoise applies an unsupervised similarity-based denoising step with a modified silhouette score to TCR-pMHC specificity data, AltraFlowSOM embeds partial expert labels into a SOM framework for imaging mass cytometry, and a benchmark of 16 dimensionality reduction methods uses 12 path-preservation metrics across point-density thresholds. What is new is the explicit treatment of model specification as the locus of error, rather than any single algorithm.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-10"
}
